case-organizer (4.8.2) stable; urgency=high

  * URGENT login fix. The session cookie was Secure-by-default in production,
    so on a plain-HTTP LAN deployment (reached by IP, not a secure context)
    the browser silently dropped it and login looped back to a blank login
    page. Secure is now OPT-IN via CASEORG_COOKIE_SECURE=1 (for HTTPS/TLS
    deployments); HSTS is only advertised over actual HTTPS requests.
  * Event modal redesigned for symmetry. The floating, centred "All day",
    "Repeat" and "Continuing" controls are gone. All-day is a plain
    left-aligned toggle; timing/recurrence read like every other field.
  * Task/deadline recurrence is now automatic: an all-day task or deadline
    repeats every day until marked complete; a fixed-time one is a one-off.
    No manual "Continuing" checkbox — the modal shows the behaviour as a note.
    Explicit recurrence (daily/weekly/monthly/yearly) remains for meetings.
  * The home action cards (Create / Manage Case, Upload / Search Case Law)
    now toggle: clicking the active card again closes it and clears the form.
  * Create Case — appellate & to-be-transferred forums. "In Appeal" now takes
    a State + District + FREE-TEXT Court/Forum (appeals often go from a
    Magistrate to a District/Sessions Court, not only a High Court), matching
    the Original Court/Forum. New "To be transferred" status for a pending
    transfer. Mirrored in the View/Edit Note editor.
  * Case Law Upload and Search now use the same grouped, searchable subcategory
    taxonomy (FILE_SUBCATS) as Manage Case, not the old flat Case Type list.
  * Reminders — "Repeating before" redesigned. You pick the lead first ("from
    30 min / 1 hour / 12 hours / 1 day / 1 week / 1 month before"); only then
    does the "every" cadence appear, offering context-appropriate frequencies
    (an hour before → every 10/15/30 min; a week before → hourly / 12-hourly /
    daily; a month before → weekly / daily). Both read inline on one row.
  * Calendar dropdowns now use the exact same Long-List Dropdown as the home
    page, and the modal dropdown-positioning bug is fixed — panels are portalled
    to <body> so a transformed modal ancestor no longer throws the options list
    across the screen. The Case Law and case-note edit dropdowns get the same
    fix and now reflect the FILE_SUBCATS taxonomy.

