case-organizer (4.7) stable; urgency=medium

  * BentoPDF suite upgraded from v1.0.0 to v2.8.6, rebuilt from upstream
    source with BASE_URL=/bento/ so every baked asset, chunk-import, locale
    and worker URL resolves under the /bento/ mount (the official release
    dist is built for a site root and breaks when embedded). Six new tools:
    Add Page Labels, Bates Numbering (useful for court bundles), Overlay
    PDF, PDF to CBZ, PDF Workflow (chain multiple operations), and
    Timestamp PDF (RFC 3161). Seven new interface locales and updated
    PDF.js viewers.
  * Bento tool links fixed under the wrapper: v2.8.6's JS renders the tools
    directory with site-root links (/merge-pdf.html) that bypassed /bento/.
    The wrapper now rewrites such navigations on click, and a server-side
    404 fallback redirects any root-level tool page to its /bento/
    counterpart — covering search results and direct URLs too. Verified in
    the browser: tools directory, tool click-through, Compress and OCR
    pages load with no console errors or failed requests.
  * Bento CSP: connect-src now also allows rawcdn.githack.com (CJK fallback
    fonts) and freetsa.org (default RFC 3161 timestamp authority for the new
    Timestamp PDF tool). Both sit behind the existing CASEORG_BENTO_CDN=0
    hardening toggle, which strips every external origin.
  * The bento tree no longer carries precompressed .br/.gz siblings — the
    app serves files through Waitress, which never used them. This removes
    ~90 MB of dead weight from the install without any behaviour change.
  * Native Windows support: new one-click EXE installer
    (CaseOrganizer-Setup-<version>.exe) built with PyInstaller + Inno Setup.
    Two install modes — a per-user tray app (no admin rights; Open / Open
    data folder / Quit menu, optional start-with-Windows) and an
    administrator "Windows service" mode (WinSW-wrapped, starts at boot
    before login, auto-restarts on failure; shortcuts open the browser at
    the running server). Tesseract OCR (+eng), Poppler, qpdf and
    Ghostscript are bundled, so the PDF suite has full feature parity with
    Linux out of the box. Localhost-only binding (127.0.0.1, ports
    5000-5010 scanned), data under %APPDATA%\CaseOrganizer (tray) or
    %ProgramData%\CaseOrganizer (service); uninstall preserves all data.
  * Windows portability fixes that also apply on Linux: Ghostscript is now
    found via gs/gswin64c/gswin32c; case, rename and calendar names are
    validated by a shared validate_fs_component() that additionally rejects
    Windows-reserved device names (CON, COM1, nul.txt, ...) and names
    ending in a dot or space — previously-accepted names like "X v. Ltd."
    now return a clear error instead of silently creating a folder Windows
    cannot read; Manage-Case subcategories and case-law fragments are
    sanitized to the same rules; the setup wizard prefills a sensible
    storage folder on Windows (Documents\Case Organizer Files, or
    ProgramData for service installs).
  * New CI workflow (.github/workflows/windows-installer.yml — the repo's
    first): on every published release it runs the full pytest suite on
    windows-latest as a parity gate, builds the installer with the same
    windows/build.ps1 maintainers use locally (vendor downloads pinned by
    SHA256 and cached), and attaches the EXE to the release.
  * Tests: suite now 277 tests, including Windows-name validation, the
    Ghostscript binary shim, and setup-prefill behaviour.

